ticket_data.description:
Browsers now enforce two more cookie name prefixes, `__Http-` and `__Host-Http-`, from the IETF cookies draft ([https://httpwg.org/http-extensions/draft-ietf-httpbis-layered-cookies.html draft-ietf-httpbis-layered-cookies], section "The `__Host-Http-` prefix"): such a cookie must have the `Secure` and `HttpOnly` attributes, and a `__Host-Http-` one also `Path=/` and no `Domain`. In the draft's "Store a Cookie" algorithm, a cookie that fails these requirements is dropped (step 16) before the step that replaces an existing cookie (step 19), and an expired cookie sent to delete one is no exception. Chrome and Edge 140 and Firefox 143 enforce this ([https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Set-Cookie MDN]).

`HttpResponse.delete_cookie()` adds `Secure` for `__Secure-` and `__Host-` names (#28989), but it never sends `HttpOnly`. A browser that enforces these prefixes therefore ignores the deletion and keeps the cookie.

This matters for the session cookie. RFC 10017 (BCP 212, "OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Applications"), section 6.1.3.2, recommends the `__Host-Http-` prefix for the session cookie of a backend that runs the login. With `SESSION_COOKIE_NAME = "__Host-Http-sessionid"`, `SessionMiddleware` deletes an emptied session's cookie (after `logout()`, for example) through `delete_cookie()`, and Firefox logs:

{{{
Cookie "__Host-Http-sessionid" has been rejected for invalid prefix.
}}}

The session row is deleted, so the leftover cookie opens nothing. But it stays in the browser until a new session replaces it, and meanwhile the middleware sends the same rejected deletion on every response that reads the session.

Steps to reproduce (Django 5.2.18, and the same code on main):

{{{
#!python
# settings.py
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME = "__Host-Http-sessionid"
SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True
# SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY = True (the default)
}}}

Over HTTPS in Firefox 143 or Chrome 140, log in and log out. The logout response carries:

{{{
Set-Cookie: __Host-Http-sessionid=""; expires=Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 GMT; Max-Age=0; Path=/; SameSite=Lax; Secure
}}}

There is no `HttpOnly`; the console reports the rejection and the cookie remains in storage.

A possible fix, by analogy with #28989: `delete_cookie()` also sets `httponly=True` when the name starts with `__Http-` or `__Host-Http-`. An alternative is an `httponly` argument that `SessionMiddleware` fills from `SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY`, as #31790 did for `samesite`, but the prefix rule covers every caller.

